ush, and B5 is a middle tile (5) so less dangerous to discard for others' chows compared to terminals.  \nDiscarding T7 would break a potential concealed pung (which gives points), so not good yet.  \nDiscarding T3 would break a pair and lose a possible eye.  \nDiscarding F2 would keep all suited tiles but F2 is a wind (South) which could be used for Prevalent/Seat wind pung if I draw another F2 (3 left unshown), but Half Flush allows honors, so F2 could be kept.  \n\nGiven remaining W tiles: I need W tiles to form melds. W8, W6, W5 \u2014 maybe chow W5-W6-W7 (but W7 scarce). Could aim for pung of W5, W6, or W8 (each has 1\u20132 left). That\u2019s risky.  \n\nBest immediate move: **Discard a non-W tile that\u2019s relatively safe and keeps flexibility.** B5 is safest among non-W because:  \n- B5\u2019s unshown count high (4), but others haven\u2019t picked many.  \n- No sign of B chows from others\u2019 histories.  \n- If I keep T3 pair and T7 triplet, I can later change plan to All Pungs (Half Flush less certain).  \n\nSo **Play B5** now, keep T7 triplet, T3 pair, and W5, W6, W8, F2, aim to draw W tiles or another F2.\n\n**Answer:** Play B5"}
